    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Harun al-Rashid became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> in 786, in his early twenties, with the help of his mother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-khayzuran">al-Khayzuran</a> and of Yahya ibn Khalid the Barmakid, whose family then ran the administration for seventeen years.</li><li>Under him the Abbasid empire reached its peak and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> flourished, while he fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, exchanged embassies with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> and put down Kharijite and Alid revolts.</li><li>He destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barmakids">Barmakids</a>, imprisoned Alid leaders, and divided the succession between his sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-amin">al-Amin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">al-Ma'mun</a>, who went to war after his death.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 802 Harun sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> an elephant named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abul-abbas">Abul-Abbas</a> and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/water-clock">water clock</a> that marked the hours by dropping bronze balls into a bowl while mechanical knights, one for each hour, came out of little doors. Charlemagne and his retinue took the clock for a conjuration.</p></aside>
    </section>

