    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">782</span><span class="rf-text">Harun nominally leads an expedition against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> that reaches the Asian suburbs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, and is given the name al-Rashid</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">786</span><span class="rf-text">On the death of his brother al-Hadi he becomes the fifth Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, with Yahya ibn Khalid the Barmakid as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">792</span><span class="rf-text">The Alid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yahya-ibn-abdallah">Yahya ibn Abdallah</a> proclaims a revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daylam">Daylam</a>, and Harun has his son Muhammad sworn in as heir with the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-amin">al-Amin</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">796</span><span class="rf-text">He moves the court and government from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raqqa">Raqqa</a> on the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">800</span><span class="rf-text">He recognises <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-i-ibn-al-aghlab">Ibrahim ibn al-Aghlab</a> as semi-autonomous emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a> in exchange for an annual payment</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">802</span><span class="rf-text">He sends <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> an elephant and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/water-clock">water clock</a>, and the new Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikephoros-i">Nikephoros I</a> refuses him tribute</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">803</span><span class="rf-text">By al-Tabari's dating, he has Ja'far the Barmakid executed and the rest of the family imprisoned</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">809</span><span class="rf-text">He dies at Tus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> while marching against the rebel Rafi ibn al-Layth, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-amin">al-Amin</a> succeeds him</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Abū Jaʿfar Hārūn ibn Muḥammad was born at Rey, then part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jibal">Jibal</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. The date is debated: sources give years from 763 to 766, with 17 March 763 and February 766 the most probable. His father was al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a>, the third Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, who reigned from 775 to 785; his mother, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-khayzuran">al-Khayzuran</a>, was a woman of strong and independent personality who greatly influenced affairs of state in the reigns of her husband and her sons.</p><p>Growing up, Harun studied history, geography, rhetoric, music, poetry and economics, but most of his time went to mastering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadith</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a>. He was also trained in swordplay, archery and the art of war. The court he was raised in defined orthodoxy by force: al-Mahdi held that the caliph had the responsibility to define the orthodox theology of Muslims, ordered polemical works against freethinkers and heretics, and for years had those suspected of being zindiqs, or dualists, hunted down and put to death in large numbers.</p><p>In 780 and again in 782, before he was caliph, Harun nominally led campaigns against the caliphate's traditional enemy, the Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, then ruled by the empress Irene. The second expedition was a huge undertaking that reached the Asian suburbs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. According to the Muslim chronicler al-Tabari, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> lost tens of thousands of soldiers and Harun used 20,000 mules to carry the riches back. In 781–82 he married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zubaidah-bint-jafar">Zubaidah</a>, the daughter of his paternal uncle Ja'far and a granddaughter of al-Mansur, the founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The raids raised Harun's political standing. On his return he was given the laqab al-Rashid, "the Rightly-Guided One", was promoted to crown prince, and was made governor of the empire's western territories, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>. When al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a> died in 785, Harun's brother al-Hadi became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>. Al-Hadi reigned for a year and two months. He clashed with their mother over her influence at court, where commanders and petitioners lined up at her door, and threatened to behead any of his men found there; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-khayzuran">al-Khayzuran</a> swore never to speak to him again and left the palace. He also tried to depose Harun as crown prince in favour of his own son Ja'far, while their mother favoured Harun.</p><p>Al-Tabari notes varying accounts of al-Hadi's death in 786, among them an abdominal ulcer and an assassination prompted by his mother. That night al-Khayzuran released Yahya ibn Khalid from prison and ordered him to pay the army's wages, send letters to the governors and prepare Harun as caliph. The commander Khuzayma ibn Khazim reportedly armed 5,000 of his followers, dragged Ja'far ibn al-Hadi from his bed and forced him to renounce his claim publicly.</p><p>Harun became caliph in his early twenties. He named Yahya, his mentor and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barmakids">Barmakid family</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> with full executive powers; Yahya and his sons served him for seventeen years. Until her death in 789 al-Khayzuran acted as an overseer of affairs, and Yahya deferred to her advice. Harun released many of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alids">Alids</a> his brother had imprisoned and declared an amnesty for all political groups of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wisdom"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-wisdom.jpg" alt="House of Wisdom" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wisdom">House of Wisdom</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maqamat_hariri.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Zereshk, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Under Harun the Abbasid empire reached its peak. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> flourished as a centre of knowledge, culture and trade, and tribute paid by many rulers funded architecture, the arts and court luxuries; he is credited with establishing the library <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wisdom">Bayt al-Hikma</a>, the "House of Wisdom". In 796 he moved the court to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raqqa">Raqqa</a> on the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>, close to the Byzantine border and well connected to Baghdad and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, and spent twelve years there. He gave the jurist Abu Yusuf the title of Grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadi">Qadi</a>, the first time it was conferred, with the power to appoint other judges. He made the pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> several times, the last in 803.</p><p>Large-scale hostilities broke out with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikephoros-i">Nikephoros I</a> became emperor in 802 and refused to pay tribute, Harun wrote his answer on the back of the emperor's letter, addressing him as "dog of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>"; after campaigns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> Nikephoros was forced to accept a treaty on humiliating terms. A Frankish mission came to offer friendship in 799, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/einhard">Einhard</a> and Notker the Stammerer describe envoys travelling between Harun and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> to discuss Christian access to holy sites. In 802 Harun sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> chessmen, an elephant named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abul-abbas">Abul-Abbas</a> and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/water-clock">water clock</a>. He also sent embassies to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a>, with which an alliance against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetans">Tibetans</a> was established.</p><p>In 800 he recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-i-ibn-al-aghlab">Ibrahim ibn al-Aghlab</a> as emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a>, semi-autonomous in exchange for 40,000 gold dinars a year. Al-Tabari reports the saying that at Harun's death the state treasury held more than nine hundred million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dirham">dirhams</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The reign saw repeated revolts. In 794 the Kharijite al-Walid ibn Tarif overran much of the Jazira and defeated two armies sent against him, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazid-ibn-mazyad-al-shaybani">Yazid ibn Mazyad al-Shaybani</a> killed him in single combat in late 795. The Alid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yahya-ibn-abdallah">Yahya ibn Abdallah</a> proclaimed a revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daylam">Daylam</a> in 792. Harun sent the Barmakid al-Fadl ibn Yahya with 50,000 men, and Yahya surrendered in return for a letter of pardon written in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>'s own hand. Harun remained suspicious. He later had Yahya brought back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and held in prison or under house arrest, and convened jurists to reassess the pardon; when Muhammad al-Shaybani declared it inviolable, the caliph threw his inkstand at him, and another judge declared it invalid and cut it with a knife. According to Shia belief, Harun also imprisoned and poisoned Musa ibn Ja'far, the seventh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imam</a>, who died in a Baghdad prison in 799.</p><p>The fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barmakids">Barmakids</a> is the most debated act of his reign. The family was imprisoned and its lands confiscated in 798, or by al-Tabari's dating in 803, and Ja'far ibn Yahya was executed; one account links the brutal manner of his death to his having let Yahya ibn Abdallah escape. The true causes are not known with certainty; it may be that Harun wanted to rid himself of a group that could limit his own power.</p><p>In Khurasan the governor Ali ibn Isa oppressed the province through heavy taxation and amassed a vast fortune. When Harun went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rayy">Rayy</a> in April 805 to investigate the complaints, Ali brought an enormous treasure, and the caliph kept him in place.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-mamun.jpg" alt="al-Ma'mun" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">al-Ma'mun</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_the_Abbasid_Caliph_al-Ma%27mun.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Classical Numismatic Group; [1], CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ali ibn Isa's misconduct in Khurasan ended in a major rebellion under Rafi ibn al-Layth, which began in Samarqand. Harun dismissed and arrested Ali and replaced him with Harthama ibn A'yan, but the revolt continued unchecked, and in 808 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> marched east himself. He fell ill and died soon after reaching the village of Sanabad in Tus, on 24 March 809. He was buried in the Dar al-Imarah, the summer palace of Humayd ibn Qahtaba, the Abbasid governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>. Shortly before his death he is said to have been reading lines by the poet Abu al-Atahiya on the transitory nature of the power and pleasures of this world, an anecdote also told of other caliphs. Inan, a singer and poet among his concubines, accompanied him to Khurasan and died there soon after him.</p><p>The succession had been arranged long before. His son Abdallah, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">al-Ma'mun</a>, was born in September 786, on the night of his accession, to Marajil, a Persian concubine who died in the birth; Muhammad, born about six months later to his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zubaidah-bint-jafar">Zubaidah</a>, took precedence because both his parents were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a>. In 792 Harun had Muhammad receive the oath of allegiance under the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-amin">al-Amin</a>, "the Trustworthy". Abdallah was named second heir, as al-Ma'mun, "the Trusted One", only in 799, and a third son, al-Qasim, was nominated third heir. Both elder heirs grew up at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raqqa">Raqqa</a>.</p><p>Al-Amin succeeded his father in 809 and ruled until 813. When he tried to remove al-Ma'mun, then governor of Khorasan, as his heir, civil war broke out between the brothers. Al-Ma'mun became caliph in 813 and ruled until 833.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/one-thousand-and-one-nights"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/one-thousand-and-one-nights.jpg" alt="One Thousand and One Nights" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/one-thousand-and-one-nights">One Thousand and One Nights</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sughrat.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Al-Mubashshir ibn Fatik, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Harun's reign is traditionally regarded as the beginning of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-golden-age">Islamic Golden Age</a>. All the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a> after him were his descendants: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-amin">al-Amin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">al-Ma'mun</a> were followed by another of his sons, Abu Ishaq Muhammad, better known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutasim">al-Mu'tasim</a>, whose mother was the concubine Marida. Harun is sometimes accused of weakening the Abbasid legacy by dividing the central powers of the realm between two sons, which created the tension that led to war between them. The place where he was buried later became known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mashhad">Mashhad</a>, "the Place of Martyrdom", after the martyrdom there of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imam</a> al-Ridha in 818.</p><p>The historian al-Masudi wrote that the splendour, riches and prosperity of the reign were such that the period was called "the Honeymoon", and the poet and musician al-Mawsili likened his accession to the sun coming out of hiding. Many anecdotes attached themselves to Harun in the centuries after his rule: al-Masudi relates a number in The Meadows of Gold, and Saadi of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a> put others into his Gulistan.</p><p>Portions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-thousand-and-one-nights">One Thousand and One Nights</a> are set at his court, some of its stories involve Harun himself, and the character of King Shahryar may have been based on him. Because of these tales he became a legendary figure, which has obscured his historical personality. He appears in poems by Alfred Tennyson, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-wadsworth-longfellow">Henry Wadsworth Longfellow</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-b-yeats">W. B. Yeats</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-joyce">James Joyce</a>'s Ulysses, and in the names of the two protagonists of Salman Rushdie's Haroun and the Sea of Stories. He has been described as one of the most famous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> in history.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Harun al-Rashid ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> from 786 to 809, when the empire reached its peak and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> flourished. He fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, exchanged gifts with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>, destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barmakids">Barmakid family</a> that had governed for him, and left a divided succession. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-thousand-and-one-nights">One Thousand and One Nights</a> made him a figure of legend.</em></p>

